<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karma Kagyu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from 12th century</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karma <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kagyu">Kagyu</a> is a major school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetan-buddhism">Tibetan Buddhism</a>, a branch of the Kagyu tradition founded in the twelfth century by the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karmapa">Karmapa</a>, Düsum Khyenpa. Its Karmapa lamas began the Tibetan practice of recognised reincarnation. The Rinpungpa and Tsangpa rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsang">Tsang</a> were its patrons, and its rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> school ended in Gelug victory in 1642.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karma-kagyu.jpg" alt="Karma Kagyu" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Secretlondon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:16_Karmapa_Statue_cf_09.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
